{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"reasoning","text":"Python 3.14.5, pandas 3.0.5, numpy 2.5.2, pydeseq2 0.5.0 are installed.\n\npandas 3.0 — PyDESeq2 0.5.0 may have compatibility issues with pandas 3.0 (e.g., `DataFrame.append` has been removed, `fillna(method=...)` removed, etc.). We'll find out when we run it. Let's write analysis.py and run it.\n\nFirst, let's quickly verify the selected samples and the raw data of the count columns.\n\nActually, let's first do a quick exploratory check with a small Python script to confirm the columns exist and the shape, then write the final analysis.py. To follow \"start with the simplest method within the first 5 tool calls\" — we're at about tool call 3 now. Let's write analysis.py directly, run it, and debug as needed.\n\nContents of analysis.py:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python\n\"\"\"Differential expression: Cisplatin_IC50_CBD_IC50 vs DMSO (PyDESeq2 0.5.0).\"\"\"\n\nimport json\nimport os\nimport sys\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport pydeseq2\nfrom pydeseq2.dds import DeseqDataSet\nfrom pydeseq2.ds import DeseqStats\n\nINPUT_DIR = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), \"..\", \"inputs\")\nOUTPUT_DIR = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))\n\nNUMERATOR = \"Cisplatin_IC50_CBD_IC50\"   # combination treatment\nDENOMINATOR = \"DMSO\"\n\ndef main():\n    # ---- Load inputs ----\n    counts = pd.read_csv(os.path.join(INPUT_DIR, \"counts_raw_unfiltered.csv\"), index_col=0)\n    layout = pd.read_csv(os.path.join(INPUT_DIR, \"sample_layout.csv\"))\n    gene_map = pd.read_csv(os.path.join(INPUT_DIR, \"ensg_to_gene_name.tsv\"), sep=\"\\t\")\n    gene_map = gene_map.dropna(subset=[\"gene_name\"]).drop_duplicates(subset=[\"ENSG\"], keep=\"first\")\n\n    # layout SampleID uses dashes (\"9-1\"), counts columns use underscores (\"9_1\")\n    layout[\"count_col\"] = layout[\"SampleID\"].str.replace(\"-\", \"_\", regex=False)\n\n    # ---- Select the six samples of the frozen contrast ----\n    sel = layout[layout[\"Group\"].isin([NUMERATOR, DENOMINATOR])]\n    assert len(sel) == 6, sel\n    sample_cols = sel[\"count_col\"].tolist()\n    missing = [c for c in sample_cols if c not in counts.columns]\n    assert not missing, missing\n\n    counts6 = counts.loc[:, sample_cols].astype(np.int64)\n\n    # ---- Prefilter: keep genes with raw count > 10 in at least one of the six samples ----\n    keep_mask = (counts6 > 10).any(axis=1)\n    counts6 = counts6.loc[keep_mask]\n    print(f\"Genes before prefilter: {keep_mask.size}, after: {len(counts6)}\")\n\n    # ---- Metadata ----\n    metadata = sel.set_index(\"count_col\").loc[sample_cols, [\"Group\"]].copy()\n    metadata[\"Group\"] = pd.Categorical(metadata[\"Group\"], categories=[DENOMINATOR, NUMERATOR])\n\n    # ---- PyDESeq2 ----\n    dds = DeseqDataSet(\n        counts=counts6.T,          # PyDESeq2 wants samples x genes\n        metadata=metadata,\n        design_factors=\"Group\",\n        ref_level=[\"Group\", DENOMINATOR],\n        refit_cooks=True,\n    )\n    dds.deseq2()\n    stat_res = DeseqStats(dds, contrast=[\"Group\", NUMERATOR, DENOMINATOR])\n    stat_res.summary()\n\n    res = stat_res.results_df.copy()\n    ...\n```\n\nWait — one important point about PyDESeq2 0.5.0 and pandas 3.0. Potential incompatibilities:\n1. PyDESeq2 uses `.astype(\"category\")` etc. — no problem.\n2. Does `pydeseq2.utils` use `pd.DataFrame.append`? I don't think so.\n3. It uses `np.log2` etc. — no problem.\n4. pandas 3.0 removed `DataFrame.fillna(method=...)` — PyDESeq2 0.5.0 was released around mid-2024 (before pandas 3.0, which is early 2025?). pandas 3.0 was released in 2025. PyDESeq2 0.5.0 may use `.fillna(method=\"ffill\")`? Hmm, uncertain.\n\nAnyway, let's run it and see. If we run into pandas 3 incompatibilities, we may need to downgrade pandas... but that affects the environment. We're in the workspace with network access and can pip install a compatible version. But wait — pandas 3.0.5 is installed; downgrading pandas might break other things, but this is the standard approach for this benchmark environment. Let's first try running with what we have.\n\nPotential PyDESeq2 issues: the check for integer counts: `if not (counts.values % 1 == 0).all()` or is_integer_dtype. Our int64 is fine.\n\nAnother potential issue: pandas 3.0 has copy-on-write as default, and string dtype is also default. PyDESeq2 might use `.str` operations on the metadata columns — no problem.\n\nAlso `DeseqStats.summary()` computes `self.results_df` and has attributes `padj`, `pvalue`, etc. Independent filtering: does 0.5.0 have `alpha`? The default alpha=0.1 for independent filtering? DeseqStats.__init__(dds, alpha=0.1, ...) — alpha is used as the significance threshold for optimizing the independent filtering threshold. This is internal; the task doesn't specify alpha, so use the default.\n\nNow, about the results_df index: Ensembl gene IDs. Add gene_name via merge with gene_map.\n\nCSV columns: gene_id (index), gene_name, baseMean, log2FoldChange, lfcSE, stat, pvalue, padj. Keep NaN padj as empty (pandas to_csv writes NaN as empty by default). Good — \"preserve unavailable adjusted p-values as null, do not convert to zero\": in CSV, empty cell = null (per the README: \"statistics missing due to independent filtering are represented as empty CSV cells or JSON null\").\n\nsummary.json: build dict with Python types; convert NaN to None. Keys:\n\n```\n{\n  \"task\": \"ls07-combination-treatment-deg\",\n  \"contrast\": {\"numerator\": \"Cisplatin_IC50_CBD_IC50\", \"denominator\": \"DMSO\"},\n  \"design\": \"~Group\",\n  \"samples\": {\"Cisplatin_IC50_CBD_IC50\": [\"9-1\",\"9-2\",\"9-3\"], \"DMSO\": [\"3-1\",\"3-2\",\"3-3\"]},\n  \"software\": {\"pydeseq2\": \"0.5.0\", \"refit_cooks\": true},\n  \"filtering\": {\"rule\": \"keep gene if raw count > 10 in at least one of the 6 samples\", \"genes_before\": X, \"genes_after\": Y},\n  \"thresholds\": {\"padj\": 0.05, \"abs_log2FoldChange\